  <h2>What the meta-analyses found for strength</h2>
  <p>Three pooled analyses are worth knowing. None of them is about the bench press alone, and most of the trials were short, so treat them as the best available guide and not a verdict.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Grgic and colleagues (2018)</strong> pooled 22 studies. Strength gains rose with frequency: effect sizes of 0.74, 0.82, 0.93 and 1.08 for training 1, 2, 3 and 4 or more times a week. But in the studies where volume was equal between groups, the effect of frequency was not significant. The gap in the raw numbers is therefore most likely about extra volume, which is our reading of the result and not the authors' wording.</li>
    <li><strong>Ralston and colleagues (2018)</strong> pooled 12 studies and reached the same place. Across all lifts there was no significant effect of frequency, and volume-equated studies showed essentially none. For upper-body lifts, three or more days a week did beat one day a week by a meaningful margin, while two days a week was no different from one. The summary does not say whether those upper-body comparisons were volume-matched.</li>
    <li><strong>Ralston and colleagues (2017)</strong> looked at weekly sets instead of days. Across nine studies, lifters doing a high number of weekly sets gained more strength than those doing a low number, with medium in between. The authors describe a graded dose-response.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Put together: more frequent training tends to help mostly because it makes room for more total work. If you can fit the same weekly sets into fewer sessions and recover, you give up little.</p>

  <h2>What they found for muscle size</h2>
  <p>Schoenfeld and colleagues (2016) pooled ten studies on frequency and muscle growth. Higher frequency was linked to a bigger effect (0.49 versus 0.30). When they looked at studies that matched volume, training a muscle twice a week produced better growth than once a week. They could not say whether three times a week beats twice. If building chest size matters to you, aim to hit the chest at least twice a week.</p>

