  <p class="chart-note">These effect sizes come from studies that did not all match weekly volume. They are not a promise of what your bench will do.</p>

  <h2>Jordan's view: recovery sets the limit</h2>
  <p>In his coaching interview, Jordan said frequency depends on goal, schedule, training age and experience. His points:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Frequency is based on how quickly you can recover, and it cannot outpace recovery if you want continued quality progress.</li>
    <li>For strength, a higher frequency that you can recover from easily is favorable. Hypertrophy is more about recovering as fully as possible from every session.</li>
    <li>For most people, 3 to 5 training days a week in total is enough. He splits a 3 day week into full-body sessions and a 4 or 5 day week into push, pull and legs, upper and lower, or by muscle group.</li>
    <li>As long as you can keep up the schedule, recover and keep making gains, that is the right one. You need to do the sessions well and have energy in them.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>One way to read this next to the research: a higher frequency helps strength when it lets you do more quality work than you could otherwise. It does not help if it leaves you too tired to train well.</p>

